  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and cool weather is expected through the rest of \nthe week, with patchy frost possible across the North Country and \nwestern Southern Tier at times.\n\nAn upper level trough and closed low will slowly move east across \nthe Midwest to Ohio Valley through Wednesday night. The trough will \nmove off the east coast and a coastal low will develop late in the \nweek. High pressure will remain across eastern Canada, bringing\ndry weather and continued easterly flow across the eastern \nGreat Lakes region though the work week. \n\nLow level moisture within easterly flow will support diurnally-\ndriven clouds across the Niagara Frontier to the western Southern \nTier this afternoon. Deeper moisture and forcing will remain south \nof the New York/Pennsylvania line and dry weather will continue\nacross the forecast area. An upper level ridge will build into \nthe region tonight while surface high pressure strengthens \nacross the Northeast. Mostly clear skies and light winds will \nsupport another cool night with temperatures falling to the 40s,\nupper 30s across the North Country. A light gradient wind is \nexpected to keep areas of frost from developing across the North\nCountry, however can't rule out patchy frost in areas \nsusceptible to lower temperatures, mainly across the Black River\nValley in far southern Lewis county. \n\nThe surface high will move further into the region Wednesday night. \nClear skies and light winds will support favorable radiational \ncooling conditions and result in another cool night across the \nregion. Temperatures in the 30s are likely across the Tug Hill \nregion and portions of the western Southern Tier, which may \nWarren frost headlines. Low temperatures in the low to mid 40s \nare expected across the lower elevations. \n\nHigh temperatures will gradually increase day to day through the \nwork week.",
